Id: cont007w
Severity: WARN
Category: containers
Check: check_containers
References: https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/#docker-daemon-attack-surface
Controls: CIS 4.1, 5.4; NIST AC-6, CM-7; ISO 8.2, 8.9; CE Secure configuration, User access control

A running container has the Docker or Podman socket mounted inside it
(-v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock). Whoever controls the
container can then drive the engine: start a privileged container that
mounts the host's /, and so be root on the host. Mounting it read-only
changes nothing, since the API is spoken over it, not written to it.
Dashboards, reverse proxies and CI runners often ask for it.

To fix it, give the container a socket proxy that allows only the API
calls it needs (for a reverse proxy, reading containers and events),
or run what needs the API outside a container.
